# NFT Watcher Bot - Cursor Rules

## Project Guidelines
- Avoid complex abstractions in MVP; duplication is fine if it helps clarity.
- Use Express for any web server needs
- Use SCSS for styling if web UI is added
- Keep console logging simple and informative
- Prioritize reliability over optimization

## Nut-Tree-Fork Configuration

### Browser Row Text for Rarity Monitoring
The bot now reads `innerText` from a browser row instead of capturing a screenshot.
Configure the selector or custom JavaScript in `.env`:

```text
BROWSER_APP=Brave Browser
RARITY_TEXT_SELECTOR=.activity-row
# or:
RARITY_TEXT_JS=(() => document.querySelector('.activity-row')?.innerText || '')()
```

### Tips for Finding the Right Row
1. Open the OpenSea activity page in the browser
2. Use browser dev tools to identify the row that contains the rarity number
3. Prefer a selector that points directly at the row you want to read
4. Keep the returned text short and focused on the rarity value

### Nut-Tree-Fork Best Practices
- Add delays between keyboard/mouse actions using sleep()
- Use `await` for all nut-tree-fork operations
- Test the browser text read before running the full bot
- Handle errors gracefully - automation can be flaky
- Make sure to grant accessibility permissions on macOS

### macOS Permissions Required
- Accessibility (System Preferences > Security & Privacy > Privacy > Accessibility)
- Automation (System Preferences > Security & Privacy > Privacy > Automation)
- Add Terminal or your Node.js executable to both lists

